IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc1-s7-base.l4 -- what the subsections of section 7 (offences committed outside Canada, deemed committed in Canada) share:
-- the way an act or omission can stand to an offence ("an offence", "a conspiracy or an attempt to commit", "being an accessory after the
-- fact or counselling in relation to") and two tests of the core record `Provision of the Code` (R1 of PLAN.md: an offence named by its section number).
-- Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30).
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.

§ `Section 7 -- what the subsections share`

-- 7 (2): "... would be an offence ... or ... would constitute a conspiracy or an attempt to commit an offence referred to in this subsection, or being an accessory after the fact or counselling in relation to such an offence".
-- src:334 | (e) commits an act or omission outside Canada that if committed in Canada would constitute a conspiracy or an attempt to commit an offence referred to in this subsection, or being an accessory after the fact or counselling in relation to such an offence,
@ref s 7(2)(e) (src:334-334)
DECLARE `s 7 how the act or omission relates to the offence` IS ONE OF
    `s 7 the act or omission would be the offence itself`
    `s 7 the act or omission would be a conspiracy to commit the offence`
    `s 7 the act or omission would be an attempt to commit the offence`
    `s 7 the act or omission would be being an accessory after the fact to the offence`
    `s 7 the act or omission would be counselling in relation to the offence`
    `s 7 the act or omission has none of those relations to the offence`

-- The offence is named by its section number (and, where the text names one, its subsection or paragraph, written as the core's `subsection` string: "(2)", "(a)").
@ref s 7 (a helper for the subsections that name offences by section number: no quotation)
GIVEN offence IS A `Provision of the Code`, n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 7 the offence is in section` MEANS offence's `section` EQUALS n

GIVEN offence IS A `Provision of the Code`, n IS A NUMBER, part IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 7 the offence is in the subsection or paragraph` MEANS offence's `section` EQUALS n AND offence's `subsection` EQUALS part

-- "any of sections 82.3 to 82.6": section numbers are decimals that sort in the Code's order, so a range is a test on both ends.
@ref s 7 (a helper for the subsections that name a range of sections: no quotation)
GIVEN offence IS A `Provision of the Code`, low IS A NUMBER, high IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 7 the offence is in the range of sections` MEANS offence's `section` AT LEAST low AND offence's `section` AT MOST high
